  "num_turns": null,
  "final_message": "Fixed. `session_ttl_expired` stripped the timezone from `now` but kept it on `created`. With timezone-aware input like `+00:00`, the subtraction then failed with `TypeError: can't subtract offset-naive and offset-aware datetimes`. I removed `.replace(tzinfo=None)`, so both timestamps are now parsed the same way. `format_expiry_message` is untouched.\n\n`python3 -m unittest -v test_public.py` now passes (1 test). I also checked a few extra cases by hand, and each gave the right answer:\n- 30 seconds elapsed with a 60-second TTL returns `False`.\n- Two timestamps with no timezone still work (`True`).\n- Timestamps with different offsets are compared correctly (`True`).\n\nOne limit remains: mixing a timestamp that has a timezone with one that doesn't still raises `TypeError`. The README doesn't say how that case should behave, so I left it alone.\n\nThe formatter reports the file would be reformatted. That's most likely the long `ttl_seconds` check line, which I didn't touch, so I left it as it was.\n\nDONE: Removed the one-sided `.replace(tzinfo=None)` in `session_ttl_expired` so timezone-aware timestamps compare correctly; `test_public.py` passes.",
